|-- src/
|   |-- index.ts              entry point; prints the banner and the intent
|   |-- config.ts             testnet chain table; no bundled RPC URL
|   |-- safety.ts             the posture strings, in one place
|   +-- wallet/
|       |-- connect.ts        EIP-1193 connect; public addresses only
|       |-- reads.ts          read-only chain calls; no write path at all
|       |-- intent.ts         unsigned, expiring withdrawal intents
|       |-- signing.ts        [CONSENT-GATED] EIP-712 signing wrapper
|       +-- broadcast.ts      [CONSENT-GATED] confirmed send wrapper
+-- test/
    +-- safety.test.ts        posture text + intent validation
```

The two `[CONSENT-GATED]` files are absent from a default scaffold. `index.ts`
feature-detects them with a dynamic import inside a `try`/`catch`, so the
default build stays green and the app degrades to read-only. Nothing in the
non-gated code imports them statically - there is a test that enforces this.

## Configuration

Copy `.env.example` to `.env` and fill it in locally. Both values ship empty.

| Variable | Purpose |
|---|---|
| `RPC_URL` | Read-only JSON-RPC endpoint. No endpoint is bundled and none is guessed. |
| `CHAIN_ID` | Testnet chain id. Empty falls back to Sepolia (`11155111`). |
| `INTENT_DESTINATION` | Destination used for the printed demo intent. |
| `INTENT_AMOUNT_WEI` | Amount used for the printed demo intent, in wei. |
| `INTENT_MEMO` | Memo attached to the printed demo intent. |
| `INTENT_NONCE` | Nonce attached to the printed demo intent. |

`npm start` does not read `.env` itself. Load it however you prefer:

```bash
node --env-file=.env src/index.ts
```

If you are building this for a browser rather than Node, add `"DOM"` to
`lib` in `tsconfig.json`; the wallet code is written against `globalThis` and
does not otherwise depend on Node.

## The withdrawal intent

`src/wallet/intent.ts` is the part of this project worth reading carefully.

An intent is **unsigned data**: an amount in wei, a chain id, a destination, a
nonce, an issue time, a short expiry, and a human-readable memo. The builder

- uses `BigInt` throughout and rejects `NaN`, negative, fractional and
  unsafe-integer amounts;
- validates the destination with `isAddress` and rejects a send to the
  connected address;
- caps the lifetime at one hour;
- produces a canonical serialisation with sorted keys, and a keccak256 hash of
  it, so two parties can compare intents without either of them signing;
- exposes `isIntentValid()` and `assertIntentValid()` so expiry is enforced
  rather than assumed;
- **never signs it, and never sends it.**

The printed demo intent uses
`0x000000000000000000000000000000000000dEaD` as its destination only because
the run needs a well-formed address that belongs to nobody. It is the standard
burn address, used here as a validation fixture. Do not send funds to it; if
funds arrive, they are unrecoverable.

The correspondence to the documented product grid is deliberately narrow:

> Wallet | Withdrawal intent | Expiring signed authorization record | Operational; no transfer

This scaffold implements the *unsigned, expiring* half of that record and the
"no transfer" property. The signed half is the consent-gated module, which
does not exist in a default scaffold.

## Consent-gated modules

`melodie init <dir> --template {{TEMPLATE}}` withholds these two files:

- `src/wallet/signing.ts`
- `src/wallet/broadcast.ts`

To have them written, you must pass `--{{CONSENT_FLAG}}` at generation time:

```bash
melodie init my-app --template {{TEMPLATE}} --{{CONSENT_FLAG}}
```

The flag is not an approval prompt and it is not a password. It is a statement
you are taking ownership of code that can produce signatures and put
transactions on a wire. Read the header comment at the top of each file before
you keep them.

Even with them present:

- `signing.ts` refuses to sign without an operator consent token passed by the
  caller, re-validates the intent and its expiry, and returns a signature
  without sending anything;
- `broadcast.ts` refuses without its own consent token, shows the exact
  transaction to a human, and sends only if the confirmation callback returns
  exactly `true`;
- neither file handles a private key;
- neither file is ever called by `index.ts`.

## What is deliberately not here

- No private key generation, import or storage.
- No seed phrase or mnemonic handling.
- No keystore.
- No write path in the read-only code.
- No mainnet configuration.
- No bundled RPC URL, API key, project id or secret.
- No scheduler, cron job, queue worker, retry loop or batch sender.
- No claims about revenue, users, transactions, TVL, throughput or audits.
  None of those numbers exist here because none of them are true.

## Testing

```bash
npm test
```

The suite covers the exact posture banner text in both configurations, intent
validation (zero amount, self-send, malformed address, non-integer / negative /
`NaN` amounts, expiry window, expiry enforcement), canonical serialisation
stability, and the structural invariant that no non-gated file statically
imports a gated one.

It does not need a network, a wallet, a `.env`, or any credential. If a test
needs one of those, the test is wrong.

